From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 08:36:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Run no page code while computing keys from a clone

add(), put() and cursor update() clone the value into a user-agent
defined realm and compute the keys from that clone, where no page code
can run. Servo deserializes the clone in the page's realm, and two
places still ran page code there, with the transaction active:

- The multiEntry key conversion reads each index of an Array with Get,
  so a hole reached a getter on the page's Array.prototype: its value
  became an index key, and its requests went ahead of the request being
  made (P3 review N1). P3 made the transaction inactive around the index
  keys as a stopgap; put()'s primary key was left as it was.
- The stored value was a second serialization of the clone, and
  serializing an Error reads its "name", which reaches a getter on the
  page's Error.prototype.

The multiEntry conversion now reads only the Array's own elements: in a
fresh realm a hole reads undefined, so the keys are the spec's. Key path
evaluation and the other key conversions already read only own
properties, and a clone has only data properties, so computing the
primary key and the index keys, also while an index is populated, runs
no page code, and the transaction-inactive toggle is gone. The record's
value is now the serialization that the clone was made from: one
serialization per put instead of two.

A realm of its own for the clone would also do, but needs a second
global per window and worker that can hold the DOM objects of a clone
(Blob, File, ImageData, ...).

The fork test index-keys-page-code.any.js now checks the spec's
behaviour: put() and update() call no getter of Array.prototype or
Object.prototype, a hole gives no index key, and a getter of
Error.prototype.name runs once, while the value is serialized, with the
transaction inactive. index-population.any.js's two subtests with a
getter that a population reached become one that checks that it isn't
reached.

Validation: Host SpiderMonkey release build of this commit: /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ and the IndexedDB WPT tests on cloning, key paths and key injection (bindings-inject-*, clone-before-keypath-eval, idbcursor_update_*, idbindex-multientry, idbobjectstore_add/put/createIndex, key_valid, keypath-exceptions, nested-cloning-*, reading-autoincrement-*, structured-clone*, value*) 970 of 970 subtests in all globals but serviceworker; with the previous commit's build, index-keys-page-code.any.js fails all 4 subtests in each global (Array.prototype[1]'s getter called once per put and update; Error.prototype.name's getter called twice, the second time with the transaction active, where its put succeeded) and index-population.any.js's new subtest fails (the getter's value became a unique index key).
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; found the remaining page-code paths, chose own-property reads over a second realm, rewrote the fork tests)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
